--TEST--
what `Core\IO::stdin` answers is `tainted`, so it reaches this class's own path sink only through a launderer that names it — spec § 14, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` and `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bytes are whatever the invoker attached to the program, so they are as
// far outside its own text as a command-line word is. `rule:security/sink-predicate` classifies
// a path component as an instruction, which makes every path in this class a
// sink -- and a sink is shut to this member's answer.
Core\IO::write(Core\IO::stdin(), "never written\n");

// The qualifier is the one thing about this member no runtime assertion can
// show: a plain `string` is assignable into a `tainted` target, so a case that
// only declared one would pass over a member that had dropped the mark. What
// pins it is the refusal above.
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
